\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Implications}
\label{sec:discussion:implications}

\paragraph{Depth-graduated data curation.}
Binary synthetic-data filters discard all machine-generated text indiscriminately, yet first-generation outputs may be benign or even beneficial for training \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse}.
Our depth estimates offer a finer-grained alternative: predicted depth can serve as a continuous quality proxy for importance weighting in data curation pipelines such as DSIR \cite{dsir2023} and QuRating \cite{qurating2024}.
Rather than filtering at a binary threshold, a curation system could assign importance weights that decay with estimated depth \cite{sugiyama2007_importance_weighting}, retaining shallow-generation text while down-weighting deeply recursive samples.
We note this as a conceptual contribution---empirical validation of depth-weighted curation remains future work.

\paragraph{Feature evolution tracks tail erosion.}
The feature importance analysis in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} reveals a depth-dependent shift in which features carry discriminative power.
At shallow depths (d0 vs.\ d1), \textsc{var\_surprisal} dominates, reflecting changes in second-order distributional structure---the variability of token surprisals decreases as the model's output distribution narrows.
At deeper recursions (d2 vs.\ d3 and beyond), \textsc{p90\_ppl} and kurtosis assume greater importance, capturing changes in the shape of the distribution's tail.
This progression aligns with the tail erosion theory of \citet{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursive generations preserve bulk distributional properties while eroding variability, and deeper generations progressively collapse the tail itself, consistent with the ``tale of tails'' analysis by \citet{dohmatob2024_tale_of_tails}.
The feature importance shift thus provides an empirical counterpart to these theoretical predictions.

\paragraph{PLM and statistical features are complementary, not competing.}
The end-to-end learning results (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}) reveal that PLMs and hand-crafted statistical features attend to fundamentally different aspects of text.
DeBERTa encodes surface properties (lexical diversity, $n$-gram repetition) while RF relies on perplexity distribution statistics (tail shape, surprisal variance)---with Spearman $\rho = -0.484$ and zero top-5 feature overlap.
Depth estimation is therefore not a problem where a sufficiently large PLM will eventually subsume statistical approaches; the two paradigms capture orthogonal signals.

\paragraph{Strategy-dependent degradation trajectories.}
Decoding strategy does not merely modulate the magnitude of depth degradation---it fundamentally reshapes the degradation trajectory.
Our cross-strategy results (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) reveal a three-tier hierarchy: nucleus sampling provides a stable baseline with consistent depth signal; temperature scaling ($\tau{=}0.9$) enhances separability at d0/d1 but induces d1/d2 boundary reversal (AUC as low as 0.13); top-$k$ truncation attenuates signal magnitude while preserving ordinal direction.
Crucially, nucleus and temperature sampling should not be conflated as ``soft sampling'' strategies---their effects on depth features are qualitatively opposite at the d1/d2 boundary.
Z-score normalization cannot rescue the temperature inversion: it has no effect on RF (scale-invariant by construction) and worsens LR d1v2 AUC on temperature holdouts (GPT-2~XL: $0.50 \to 0.24$; OLMo: $0.50 \to 0.19$), confirming that temperature induces a genuine structural distributional shift rather than a correctable scale artifact.
In deployment, the strategy type must be known a priori or inferred as a preprocessing step; strategy-conditioned classifiers are a natural solution, though reliable strategy identification from text alone remains an open question.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion:limitations}

\paragraph{d0 recall dominance inflates headline accuracy.}
Cross-model LOO accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially boosted by near-perfect d0 recall ($\geq$98.5\% across all holdouts), since the human vs.\ machine boundary transfers trivially.
Within generated text (d1--d3), mean accuracy drops to 55.4\%, with d2 recall as low as 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Headline accuracy therefore overstates the system's ability to resolve fine-grained depth distinctions---the more practically relevant capability.

\paragraph{Controlled experimental setting.}
All experiments use a fixed seed corpus (Wikipedia), LoRA finetuning, and three model families at 1--1.5B parameter scale.
This controlled design isolates the effect of recursive depth but leaves open how well the approach transfers to mixed-domain seed text, instruction-tuned models, full finetuning, and larger model scales (7B+).
The gap between our controlled setting and the heterogeneous conditions of real-world recursive contamination remains uncharacterized.

\paragraph{Reference model circularity.}
Our 19 statistical features are computed relative to a GPT-2 base (124M) reference model.
In the cross-model leave-one-out evaluation, the GPT-2~XL holdout shares partial architectural overlap with this reference---both belong to the GPT-2 family.
However, GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed that of the architecturally unrelated OLMo holdout (69.2\%), suggesting that architectural overlap is not the primary driver of transfer performance.
Nonetheless, the choice of reference model introduces a potential confound that warrants investigation with non-GPT-2 reference models.

\paragraph{Feature fragility under domain shift.}
\citet{pudasaini2026_detection_fails} demonstrate that detection features degrade under domain shift, with classifiers trained on one text domain failing to generalize to others.
Our ordinal depth features---derived from the same class of perplexity and entropy statistics---are likely susceptible to analogous fragility.
A depth estimator trained on Wikipedia-seeded recursive text may not transfer to recursive generation seeded from news, code, or dialogue without domain-specific recalibration.

\paragraph{Non-monotonic early-depth dynamics.}
Our EDD framework assumes that text quality degrades monotonically with recursive depth.
\citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} present evidence that early recursive iterations can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality before eventual collapse, implying that the depth--degradation relationship may be non-monotonic in the initial generations.
If feature trajectories reflect this non-monotonicity, the ordinal classification assumption---that depth classes are naturally ordered---may break down for shallow depths under certain conditions.

\paragraph{Curated synthetic pipelines.}
\citet{falahati2026_curated} show that carefully curated synthetic data does not necessarily trigger model collapse, challenging the assumption that recursive depth invariably produces distributional degradation.
In such curated pipelines, the statistical fingerprints we exploit may be absent or attenuated, rendering depth estimation infeasible.
The boundary conditions under which curation neutralizes depth signal remain unknown.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We formalized recursive generation depth estimation and showed that 19 statistical features achieve 70--85\% four-class accuracy ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$; cross-model 66.4\%, cross-strategy 59.1\%).
Future work should address wild-data transfer, larger scales, and strategy-conditioned classifiers; code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment constraint.}
Cross-strategy transfer induces a d1/d2 boundary inversion under temperature sampling.
Per-strategy z-score normalization partially corrects the inversion (GPT-2~XL d1v2 AUC $0.24{\to}0.56$, OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$) but leaves a residual gap relative to within-strategy performance (${\sim}0.83$), indicating that a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + strategy-conditioned classification) is needed for reliable cross-strategy deployment.

\paragraph{d0 inflation of headline metrics.}
Depth~0 (human-written text) is near-perfectly separable (recall $>$98\% across all settings), inflating headline accuracy.
Cross-model transfer accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially driven by d0; d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\paragraph{Controlled vs.\ ecological setting.}
All experiments use Wikipedia seed text with controlled recursive generation (LoRA fine-tuning, 1--1.5B parameter models).
Transfer to mixed-domain corpora, instruction-tuned models, and larger scales (7B+) remains open.
Wild-data calibration is probed only indirectly; ecological transfer is future work.

\paragraph{EDD monotonicity assumption.}
Our EDD definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}) requires all pairwise AUCs up to depth $d^*$ to exceed $T$, which is well-behaved when AUC decays monotonically---as observed in all our configurations.
However, \citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} show that early recursion can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality, which could produce non-monotonic AUC profiles (e.g., a dip at d1/d2 followed by recovery at d2/d3).
Our cross-strategy transfer results provide an empirical example: training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature yields d1-vs-d2 AUC as low as 0.129 (GPT-2~XL) and 0.253 (OLMo), violating the monotonicity assumption.
In such cases EDD would conservatively report a lower value than the deepest discriminable pair, potentially underestimating the true discriminable range; extending EDD to handle non-contiguous discriminable regions is future work.
The choice of threshold $T$ is also application-dependent: at $T{=}0.80$, several cells lose one depth level relative to $T{=}0.75$.

\paragraph{Reference model dependency.}
Surprisal-based features are computed relative to GPT-2 base (124M), introducing partial overlap when GPT-2~XL is held out, though GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed the unrelated OLMo (69.2\%).
The choice of reference model affects feature values; a different reference model may shift absolute feature magnitudes and potentially alter discriminability at depth boundaries.

\paragraph{Additional caveats.}
Detection features degrade under domain shift \cite{pudasaini2026_detection_fails}; our depth features are likely susceptible.
\citet{falahati2026_curated} show that curated synthetic data need not trigger collapse, attenuating the fingerprints we exploit.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available pretrained language models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2~XL) and Wikipedia text as seed data.
No human subjects were involved, no private or sensitive data were collected, and no new models were released.
Our method is intended to support data provenance and training-data auditing; we acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be misused to circumvent content moderation, though such risks are shared with all detection-oriented research.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Depth-graduated curation and deployment.}
Predicted depth can serve as a quality proxy for importance weighting \cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}, down-weighting deeply recursive samples that binary filters \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse} would indiscriminately discard.
However, cross-strategy deployment faces a significant obstacle: the temperature-induced d1/d2 boundary inversion (GPT-2~XL d1v2 AUC$\,{=}\,$0.129, OLMo$\,{=}\,$0.253; \S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) means that a classifier trained without knowledge of the decoding strategy may systematically misrank depths.
Per-strategy normalization partially corrects the scale component but leaves a residual gap that simple feature normalization cannot resolve; whether more sophisticated domain adaptation methods could recover the boundary remains open.
Reliable cross-strategy deployment therefore requires a two-stage pipeline---automatic strategy identification followed by strategy-conditioned classification---making strategy detection a prerequisite rather than an optional enhancement.

\paragraph{Mixed-depth corpora.}
Our evaluation assumes each passage is generated at a single depth, yet real-world documents may interleave human-written and machine-generated segments at different recursion depths within the same text.
Passage-level classification would assign a single depth label to such mixed documents, potentially producing misleading estimates.
Extending the framework to segment-level depth estimation---detecting depth transitions within a document---is a natural next step, though it introduces additional challenges in boundary detection and short-segment feature reliability.

\paragraph{Adversarial robustness.}
Adversarial paraphrasing and post-hoc editing could disrupt the statistical fingerprints that our method relies on, particularly the perplexity tail features.
\citet{sadasivan2023_paraphrase} demonstrate that paraphrasing attacks reduce binary detection accuracy to near chance; similar attacks would likely degrade depth estimation.
Depth estimation and watermarking \cite{kirchenbauer2023_watermark} address complementary aspects of provenance---the former operates retroactively on any text, while the latter requires proactive embedding---and combining both could provide more robust provenance tracking.

\paragraph{Data provenance.}
Depth estimation contributes one dimension to a broader data provenance toolkit.
Combined with model attribution \cite{uchendu2020_attribution} (identifying \emph{which} model generated a text) and watermarking (verifying \emph{whether} a specific model generated it), depth estimation answers a distinct question: \emph{how many recursive iterations} has the text undergone.
These complementary signals could be integrated into a unified provenance pipeline for training data auditing.

\paragraph{Scaling to larger models and deeper recursion.}
Our benchmark covers 1--1.5B parameter models at depths up to $d{=}5$.
Larger models (7B--70B+) may exhibit different collapse dynamics---potentially slower degradation due to greater capacity, or altered feature hierarchies---and deeper recursion ($d > 5$) may reveal new regimes beyond the signal saturation observed at $d{=}5$.
Extending the benchmark to these scales will require addressing computational costs of multi-generation pipelines and may necessitate more efficient feature computation than full reference-model inference.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares classification methods on the Pythia-1.4B nucleus setting.
Three feature-based methods---Random Forest (RF), CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, and DivEye \cite{diveye2026}---all operate on the same 19 statistical features and achieve statistically indistinguishable accuracy (78.1--78.2\%; pairwise bootstrap $p>0.05$ after Bonferroni correction, 95\% CI within $\pm$1.1pp).
These three significantly outperform CE-MLP ($p<0.001$, $+$3.8pp), which in turn trails logistic regression by $\sim$0.1pp.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%), confirming that depth estimation requires the multi-dimensional feature representation defined in \S\ref{sec:method}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Comparison of classification methods on Pythia-1.4B nucleus. RF, CORAL, and DivEye use the same 19 statistical features and are statistically indistinguishable (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected). Chance = 25\%. 5-fold CV std: 0.2--0.9pp across all methods.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Method & 4-class Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
RF (19 features) & 78.2 \\
CORAL & 78.1 \\
DivEye & 78.2 \\
CE-MLP & 74.9 \\
LR & 75.0 \\
DeepSeek V3.2 (zero-shot)\footnotemark & 40.0 \\
ppl-regression (single feature) & 25.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\footnotetext{Evaluated on 200 samples (approximately 50 per class) from the Pythia-1.4B nucleus cell only, due to API cost; 95\% CI: $\pm$6.8pp. All other methods use the full 20{,}000-sample set.}
\end{table}

That three structurally different classifiers achieve identical performance confirms that the discriminative signal resides in the 19 features, not the classifier architecture; a zero-shot LLM baseline (DeepSeek V3.2) reaches only 40.0\%.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 is near-perfectly identified in both settings; errors concentrate at the d2/d3 boundary in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under cross-model transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Train on 2 model families, test on held-out 3rd. Chance = 25\%. d0 recall $>$98\% inflates headline accuracy; d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.5\%.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy reaches 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, the d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.5\% across holdout conditions (Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.3\%; chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Train on 2 strategies, test on held-out 3rd. Chance = 25\%. d0 recall $\geq$89.5\% inflates headline accuracy.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%), indicating that strategy-induced shifts pose a greater challenge than architectural differences.
As with cross-model transfer, d0 recall remains high ($\geq$89.5\%), inflating headline accuracy; excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops further (XX.X\%; chance = 33\%).
d2 recall ranges from 3.7\% to 19\%, confirming mid-chain depths as the primary confusion locus.

\paragraph{Temperature-induced boundary inversion.}
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature yields d1v2 AUC below 0.5 (GPT-2~XL: 0.129, OLMo: 0.253), inverting the d1/d2 boundary (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).
Pooled standardization \cite{sugiyama2007_importance_weighting} fails (d1v2 AUC $\leq$0.24), but per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers the boundary (GPT-2~XL $0.24{\to}0.56$, OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$, Pythia $0.52{\to}0.72$), leaving a residual gap to within-strategy performance (${\sim}$0.83).

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end both converge to ${\sim}$59\% (19pp below RF 78.2\%; 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias).
Linear probing shows PLMs cannot recover the dominant depth features: \textsc{var\_surprisal} and \textsc{p90\_ppl} yield negative $R^2$ from DeBERTa hidden states (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}), yet SHAP analysis attributes 88.8\% of RF's contribution to perplexity/surprisal features.
DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached only 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}); all end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) underperform feature-based methods, confirming that depth estimation benefits from explicit distributional statistics.
